      "title": "Deploy the next anon.wei",
      "tagline": "Put anon.wei's next version on Ethereum, its catch-up registry on four chains, and the first points: 22 transactions, from any wallet.",
      "about": [
        {
          "h": "What it does",
          "p": "anon.wei opens a new device by reading the pool's whole history, which on a busy chain takes a long time. The catch-up registry holds the pool's tree edge at a count, checked against the pool when it is posted, so a new device starts from the newest point and reads the older history in the background. The next version of the page also adds wallet apps by WalletConnect and a round of fixes."
        },
        {
          "h": "What gets deployed",
          "p": "The page's 16 chunks on Ethereum (plain code the page is read from), then the registry on Ethereum, Base, Robinhood Chain and MegaETH at one address, then one catch-up point on Base and one on Robinhood Chain, where the pool's history is longest. The page's own contract and the anon.wei name are the steward's to set afterwards."
        },
        {
          "h": "Who controls it",
          "p": "Nobody. The registry has no owner and no settings: anyone may post a point, and it keeps one only if the pool itself held that root at that count. The chunks are code with no owner."
        },
        {
          "h": "Who can send it",
          "p": "Anyone. Each address is fixed in advance by the exact code, so the sender cannot change what lands there. A point is read from the chain's keeper when it is sent, and the registry refuses one the pool did not hold."
        }
      ],
